On 27.11.2011 02:44, Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> On Nov 26, 1:48 pm, Doug Freyburger <dfrey...@yahoo.com> wrote:
>>
>> I name gems like "hard green lev 4"
> 
> Is there any real point to classifying and carrying
> a bunch of gems around that early in the game?

Throwing at unicorns to distinguish precious ones from
glass, and then to increase your luck by throwing named
(non-glass) gems at a co-aligned unicorn. Then, if you
need money, identify those gems that are most valuable,
and sell them; to buy useful items or buy protection
from priests. Collecting (maybe carrying the gems with
you) will make it possible to sort out the glass because
they are the most common ones, and you can thus reduce
your weight by dropping the glass which is typically 90%
of that stuff. The priority is to throw gems from the
larger stacks first at unicorns to be able to sort out
the junk first.

> I've been leaving them where they are until at
> least mid-game, by which time I am stronger and
> can carry more and have a blessed bag of holding
> and usually also a reliable way of officially identifying
> things, not to mention teleport control, which makes
> collecting them MUCH easier.  I have to run back
> through all the levels anyway to pick up the magic
> items (scrolls and potions and whatnot) that I left
> all over the place because I couldn't bear their
> weight in the early game[1], so grabbing the gems
> while I'm at it at that point is very little extra trouble.

Of course you can trade the availability by using stashes
and travelling forth and back; it depends on the actual
game what's better. And being unburdened should always be
high priority anyway.

>> I like to carry a hard gem with me as an engraver
> 
> Now that makes sense.
> I may have to think about doing that.
> But still, you only need one for that, right?

Consider also, that a *ring* with a gemstone will serve
as a substitute for a gem. Having one or the other is
very helpful (and one is sufficient for that purpose).

>> I'll toss soft gems to unicorns
> 
> I can seldom get unicorns to hold still long
> enough to throw anything at them, especially
> in the early game. 

Nethack is not designed to be easy :-)
But speed, invisibility, jumping, displacement, and similar
may help. Once you succeeded it gets easier.

> I eventually identify them all collaterally,
> merely by holding a handful of them in my
> inventory each time I happen to be reading
> a blessed scroll of identify for some other
> reason.  In those kinds of quantities they
> weigh almost nothing (and you only need to
> hold one of each type, once each), so they
> don't significantly decrease the amount of
> other stuff you can hold, so there's no real
> downside to doing this.

Well, I usually find it better to identify other stuff,
since by engrave-testing and by unicorn-testing you get
most of the information you need.

> [1] With no bag of holding and early-game
>     strength, anything not immediately vital to
>     survival must be left.  Haha, you want
>     to wear armor AND carry both melee and
>     projectile weapons, plus food?  You'll be
>     burdened the first time you pick up a
>     couple of potions or scrolls.  Forget tools,
>     except maybe a key (or a unicorn horn, if
>     you can get your hands on one).

Actually I think this is bad advice. Tools are amongst
the most useful items in Nethack; there's a reason why
they are made heavy[*]. A stethoscope is a life saver.
The mentioned camera as well. Horns and drums, even the
non-magic ones, also. A tinning kit to get the necessary
resistances fast which may otherwise kill you instantly.
Lamps to increase survival chance in dark areas. Sacks
and bags to protect your loot. Towels and blindfolds,
once you have eaten a floating eye, to spot and identify
monsters early.

Janis

[*] Never understood, though, why orbs are so extremely
heavy compared to other tools, and they have drawbacks
as well; I leave those alone.


